{"skill": "Supporting Claims", "difficulty": "Easy", "question": "Consider the following summary about Macbeth’s shift from a noble figure to a tyrant:

In Shakespeare’s tragedy Macbeth, the title character’s initial hesitation to commit regicide dissolves under the influence of his ambition. Once crowned, Macbeth becomes increasingly paranoid, orchestrating murders to maintain his power. By the end, he has abandoned moral scruples and isolated himself from allies.

Which choice best supports the claim that Macbeth’s ambition results in his tyrannical behavior?", "choices": ["Macbeth discusses his moral doubts in a soliloquy before murdering Duncan.", "Macbeth arranges Banquo’s assassination to eliminate a perceived threat to his throne.", "Lady Macbeth encourages Macbeth to wash his hands to rid himself of guilt.", "Macduff flees Scotland to gather allies, seeking to overthrow Macbeth’s reign."], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Choice B highlights Macbeth’s active steps to eliminate potential threats and maintain his power, illustrating how his ambition drives him toward ruthless acts. Although Macbeth’s doubt (choice A) and guilt (choice C) reveal internal conflict, they do not directly show tyranny. Macduff’s decision to flee (choice D) is more about resistance to Macbeth’s rule than proof of Macbeth’s tyranny. Thus, Banquo’s assassination plan best illustrates how Macbeth’s ambition leads him to commit acts characteristic of a tyrant.", "topic": "Macbeth’s ambition and descent into tyranny in Macbeth"}

